---
title: DOCX
description: Paste Word content, import bounded DOCX files, and export an explicit document projection.
---

Use `WordPastePlugin` for clipboard content. Use `importDocx` and `exportDocx` for files. The three operations have separate entrypoints and dependencies.

<ComponentPreview name="docx-demo" />

<PackageInfo>

## Features

- Normalize HTML and RTF pasted from Microsoft Word
- Import a bounded DOCX package into one complete editor document
- Preserve supported Word revisions and comment ranges as structured data
- Export one captured editor snapshot as an accepted, proposed, or review DOCX

</PackageInfo>

<Callout type="info" title="Client boundary">
  Paste, file import, and file download use browser APIs. Keep these operations
  in client code. Export uses React static rendering, so pass the descriptors
  and static components that define your document output.
</Callout>

## Kit usage

The app-local `DocxKit` installs `WordPastePlugin`. File import and export are standalone functions.

<Steps>

### Installation

```bash
npm install platejs juice validator
```

### Add kit

<ComponentSource name="docx" />

```tsx title="components/editor/editor.tsx"
import { createEditor } from "platejs/react";
import { DocxKit } from "@/components/editor/docx";

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [...DocxKit],
});
```

</Steps>

## Paste from Word

`WordPastePlugin` inlines pasted CSS and normalizes Word HTML and RTF before the installed Plate format mappings decode the content.

```tsx title="components/editor/editor.tsx"
import { WordPastePlugin } from "platejs/docx/paste";
import { createEditor } from "platejs/react";

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: [WordPastePlugin],
});
```

Clipboard paste does not upload embedded images or preserve Word table geometry. Configure those destination policies in their owning media and table features.

## Import a DOCX file

`importDocx` reads a `Blob` or `ArrayBuffer` without modifying the editor. On success, replace the complete editor value so document metadata, named roots, and authored review state stay coherent.

Inside a mounted React control, get that complete editor with
`useModelEditor()`. `useEditor()` returns the provider-selected mounted command
view, whose root and authored view belong to that view.

```bash
npm install platejs jszip mammoth validator
```

```tsx title="components/editor/import-docx.tsx"
import { importDocx } from "platejs/docx/import";
import { createEditor } from "platejs/react";
import { BaseEditorKit } from "@/components/editor/plugins-static";

const editor = createEditor({
  plugins: BaseEditorKit,
});

export async function importFile(file: File) {
  const result = await importDocx(file, {
    plugins: BaseEditorKit,
  });

  if (!result.ok) {
    return { diagnostics: result.diagnostics };
  }

  editor.update.value.replace(result.document);

  return {
    comments: result.comments,
    diagnostics: result.diagnostics,
  };
}
```

An expected file, package, limit, or decode failure returns `{ ok: false, diagnostics }` and no document. An aborted import rejects with the signal reason. Invalid options and internal invariant failures throw.

### Lossy files

`importDocx` rejects content loss by default. Import leaves out images, footnotes, endnotes, headers and footers that hold content, and the box of a checkbox that no installed HTML mapping decodes. A text box keeps its text when the file also draws it in VML that the converter reads, and is reported either way. Each of these losses fails the import with an error diagnostic, and the result is `ok: false`. So does markup that the HTML sanitizer removes along with content a reader would see, a comment without a complete range, or a tracked revision that the installed plugins cannot keep. Set `lossPolicy: "allow"` to import the rest of the file and receive the same losses as warnings:

```tsx
const result = await importDocx(file, {
  lossPolicy: "allow",
  plugins: BaseEditorKit,
});
```

An attribute or style that an HTML mapping loses warns under both policies and never fails the import. It returns a `property-dropped` warning whose `feature` names what was lost, such as `data-list-type`, and whose `count` is the number of runs, paragraphs, or cells that lost it. A loss whose mapping names no attribute is counted by its message, so links refused for different destinations warn once each. A link keeps its label when the sanitizer refuses its destination. Unless that destination could run script, the link warns the same way. For example, a numbered Word list imported without the list plugin keeps its text as paragraphs and reports its list attributes this way. When two installed marks cannot share the same text, schema fitting keeps one and returns a lossy `schema-repair` diagnostic instead, which follows the loss policy.

```tsx
for (const diagnostic of result.diagnostics) {
  if (diagnostic.code === "property-dropped") {
    console.warn(diagnostic.feature, diagnostic.count);
  }
}
```

Every `unsupported-content` diagnostic declares `impact`. A `lossless` one never fails the import. It reports markup that import removed or unwrapped while keeping what a reader sees, such as a smart tag, a content control, a link destination that could run script, or a `<style>` element that a mapping added. One `lossless` report still loses content. Import leaves out the text inside a custom XML element.

Import neither keeps nor reports:

- formatting the converter does not read, whether direct or from a Word style, such as underline, text and background color, highlight, alignment, font size, font, and line height;
- hidden text, which imports as ordinary text;
- a header or footer part whose name does not start with `word/header` or `word/footer`;
- headings, links, and tables imported without their plugins, which become plain text.

A footnote or endnote reference with its own mark, such as `*`, leaves that mark in the text. Import leaves notes out of the text only for a note kind it reports. It reports a kind only from a part named `word/footnotes.xml` or `word/endnotes.xml`. Notes in a part under another name stay in the text with their markers and without a report. When the file also holds a default-named part of that kind with notes in it, import leaves the renamed part's notes out too, under the default-named part's report. Import reports a file entry whose name starts with `word/header` or `word/footer` but is not XML, such as a picture, as a lost header or footer. An empty header or footer paragraph counts as content when it sets a border, shading, or numbering. The setting can come from the paragraph, its style, or the document defaults, and it counts even when it draws nothing, such as a border of `none`. A checkbox also loses any label text that Word stores in the same `w:t` element as its box, under the same report.

The converter's own messages, such as one about a symbol it cannot convert, return as `converter-message` warnings and never fail the import.

### Retain the source for later export

Set `retainSource: true` when the same editor imports, edits, and exports a Word file. A successful import then includes `source`. It is a `DocxSource` when exact export can return the package unchanged. Otherwise it is `null`, with a `source-unavailable` warning that names the first part exact export cannot admit. Keep the source in application state, pass it to `exportDocx`, and dispose it when the document closes or another file replaces it.

```tsx title="components/editor/docx-roundtrip.tsx" showLineNumbers
import { exportDocx } from "platejs/docx/export";
import { importDocx } from "platejs/docx/import";

const imported = await importDocx(file, {
  plugins: BaseEditorKit,
  retainSource: true,
});

if (!imported.ok) return imported.diagnostics;

editor.update.value.replace(imported.document);

const exported = await exportDocx(editor, {
  projection: "review",
  source: imported.source,
});

imported.source?.dispose();
```

Exact export admits a closed set of passive package content:

- the main document at `word/document.xml`, styles, settings, numbering, fonts, and theme;
- headers, footers, footnotes, endnotes, and comments;
- core, extended, and custom document properties and a thumbnail;
- AVIF, BMP, GIF, JPEG, PNG, and WebP images reached through internal relationships;
- hyperlinks whose destination is absolute and meets the navigation floor.

`PAGE` is the only recognized field instruction. Any other part, relationship, markup namespace, field instruction, or XML processing instruction leaves `source` null. That includes unsafe or relative hyperlinks, other external relationships, macros, ActiveX controls, OLE objects, imported HTML chunks, external templates, custom XML data other than Plate's review part, and SVG or EMF images. A null source does not mean the import lost content; exporting without a source generates the document from editor content.

`DocxSource` retains the admitted compressed package plus immutable document, comment, schema, and limit correspondence. It does not retain expanded ZIP entries or an editor reference. `dispose()` is idempotent. An export that already acquired the source can finish after disposal; later exports report `source-unavailable` and generate a fresh DOCX.

### Import comments

The importer returns comment format facts without writing app comment storage. Each `DocxComment` can include a rich Plate `body`, an exact `target.range` that addresses the returned document, Word author and date metadata, a durable ID, a parent ID, and resolved state. Map those records to your app identities explicitly.

### Import tracked revisions

Include `AuthoredPlugin` from `platejs/authored` in `plugins` to keep Word revisions. Supported insertions, deletions, moves, run property changes, and paragraph property changes then become pending authored changes. Each change takes its Word revision's ID, as `docx-<id>`, and its author and date. A missing author or an invalid date produces a `lossy-content` warning, and an invalid date imports as time 0.

Without `AuthoredPlugin`, or when the revisions cannot map to the installed schema, the importer flattens them into the proposed document and reports a `lossy-content` warning.

A file that Plate exported for review also holds Plate's review part. The importer restores each reviewed block whose Word paragraphs are unchanged since export, with its original change IDs and the document's review records. A block whose paragraphs were edited in Word imports from its Word revisions and reports a warning.

### Import list numbering

Numbered and bulleted Word paragraphs become list items, and each item joins the list of its Word numbering definition. Word counts every instance of a definition in one sequence per level. A list therefore continues past other paragraphs and across instances, and lists of different definitions that interleave keep their own numbers. A level restarts after a paragraph at the level its `lvlRestart` names or a shallower one, which by default is the level just above it. An instance's start override resets its level once, at the instance's first paragraph at that level. That item imports with the override as `listRestart`, and the items after it count on from it, so an inserted item renumbers the rest the way Word does. Each item's `listLevels` hold its definition's level starts, `lvlRestart` rules and level text, with its instance's format and level text overrides applied. Word ignores a start or `lvlRestart` inside a level override, and so does the import. A paragraph whose instance shows its level as bullets imports as a bulleted item and does not count.

## Export a DOCX file

`exportDocx` captures the editor once before asynchronous rendering. Pass the visible projection explicitly.

```bash
npm install platejs color-name html-to-vdom jszip juice katex@0.16.22 mime-types virtual-dom xmlbuilder2
```

```tsx title="components/editor/export-docx.tsx"
import { exportDocx } from "platejs/docx/export";
import { createEditor } from "platejs/react";
import { BaseEditorKit } from "@/components/editor/plugins-static";
import { DOCX_EXPORT_STYLES } from "@/components/editor/docx-export";

const editor = createEditor({ plugins: BaseEditorKit });

export async function downloadEditorDocx() {
  const result = await exportDocx(editor, {
    orientation: "portrait",
    projection: "proposed",
    stylesheet: DOCX_EXPORT_STYLES,
    title: "Document",
  });

  if (!result.ok) return result.diagnostics;

  const url = URL.createObjectURL(result.blob);
  const link = document.createElement("a");
  link.href = url;
  link.download = "document.docx";
  document.body.append(link);
  link.click();
  link.remove();
  URL.revokeObjectURL(url);

  return result.diagnostics;
}
```

Choose `accepted` to omit pending changes, `proposed` to apply them, or `review` to write Word tracked-change markup. Accepted and proposed exports warn about the pending changes they decided.

A review file contains what Word displays: text, comments, and tracked revisions with their authors. It also stores each top-level block that holds a pending change, with the document's review records, in a custom XML part, and binds each block to its Word paragraphs with a bookmark. Importing the file restores the blocks Word left unchanged, as [Import tracked revisions](#import-tracked-revisions) describes. Export does not write named roots or document metadata to the file. To keep them, save `editor.read.value()` as JSON beside the file, as [Serializing](/docs/plate/serializing) describes.

### Content loss

`exportDocx` rejects content loss by default. When an image cannot be embedded, a supplied comment has no representable range, or a node has no drawing in `presentation`, the result is `ok: false` with the loss as an error diagnostic. Set `lossPolicy: "allow"` to download the file and receive the same loss as warnings:

```tsx
const result = await exportDocx(editor, {
  comments,
  lossPolicy: "allow",
  projection: "review",
});
```

Named roots, document metadata, removed link destinations, and other properties DOCX cannot represent are warnings under both policies. An `accepted` export omits a comment whose annotated content that projection excludes, and reports a warning, as Word does when the annotated suggestion is rejected.

A heading, list, table or other block inside a blockquote exports in its place but outside the quote. A blockquote inside a blockquote merges into the outer quote. Two blockquotes in a row come back from Word as one quote when the first ends and the second starts with a paragraph that is not a list item. Each case reports a `quote-structure` warning under both policies.

### Lists

Each Plate list becomes one Word list. Its `listLevels` give the list's level starts, `lvlRestart` rules and level text. An item with `listRestart`, or one whose number Word would not reach, gets a numbering instance of its own that overrides that level's start, as Word's Restart Numbering writes it. The items after it count on from it in the list's own instance. Word and a re-import then show the numbers the editor shows, and the restart stays on that item. Word draws one marker style per list level, so an item whose `listStyle` differs from the first item at its level keeps that first style and reports a `list-marker` warning under both policies. A Word list level is numbered or unordered, as its first item is, and the exporter writes one set of level rules per list. Bullets and to-dos share unordered levels, and every to-do takes the document's shared to-do numbering. A numbered item at an unordered level, or the reverse, is therefore not part of its list in Word, and an item whose `listLevels` differ from its list's first item takes that item's rules. A kind split reports a `list-structure` warning, and so does a numbered or task item whose `listLevels` differ from its list's first numbered or task item; `lossPolicy: 'reject'` fails the export. A bullet without `listId`, as Markdown and AI output write it, stays out of the kind check, because a Markdown bullet list never shares a list with a numbered one. Word has no start that applies only when an item opens its level, and it takes each level's start from the list's first item, 1 by default. A numbered or task item whose `listStart` differs from the start its list's first numbered or task item gives that level exports its shown number and reports a `list-start` warning under both policies. Bullets take no part in the level-rule and start checks, so a bullet that leads its list gives Word its level rules and starts without a warning.

A heading or blockquote that is a list item keeps its heading style and bookmark or its `Quote` style, and takes its number and indent from its list level. A numbered blockquote exports as one numbered paragraph that joins the text of the blocks it holds. Export warns when it holds more than one paragraph or any other block. A table cell exports its blocks as the body does, and a nested table and its images fit the cell that holds them.

### Links and images

Export writes a link destination only when it is absolute and meets the navigation floor, such as `https:` or `mailto:`. It writes bookmark links such as `#introduction` as Word anchors. An unsafe destination, or a relative link that has no document base in a Word file, keeps its text without the destination.

Images are embedded as PNG, JPEG, GIF, or BMP. Set `allowRemoteImages: true` to fetch HTTP(S) images during export. These images are omitted and leave their alt text:

- an image with an unsafe, relative, or blob source;
- a remote image when `allowRemoteImages` is off;
- an image that cannot be fetched;
- an image in any other format.

Before returning a generated file, export checks the written package against the same passive vocabulary that retained sources use. Output outside it is withheld with an `invalid-package` error. An unchanged source-aware export returns the retained file, which passed the same check when it was imported.

### Source-aware export

With a valid `source`, an unchanged `review` export returns the admitted file byte for byte. Exact reuse requires the same compiled schema, an unchanged review document, unchanged or omitted comments, and no `title`, `margins`, `orientation`, `pageSize`, `stylesheet`, or `fontFamily` option. `component` and `presentation` do not apply on this path, because export regenerates nothing.

If an exact check fails, `exportDocx` regenerates the complete document body. It keeps these source units when their internal relationships are closed, reachable, and nonconflicting:

- headers and footers from a document with one section, including their images and hyperlinks;
- custom document properties.

The regenerated package has its own core document properties. It omits source body XML, body-only parts such as footnotes and body images, and headers and footers from a document with multiple sections. It also omits the source core and extended properties and the package thumbnail: the core properties, such as the author, title and creation date, the extended properties, which count pages and words and hold details such as the company name, and the thumbnail, which pictures the body. Diagnostics report why the source was rewritten and identify each omitted source part. A custom property linked to body text keeps the value cached in the source file, even when the edit changed that text.

When regeneration is required, omitted `comments` means that no comments are generated. Pass the current app-owned comment set explicitly to retain comments after an edit. Imported comment ranges are not relocated automatically.

### Export comments

Pass comments whose ranges address the editor's stored document, the value `editor.read.value()` returns. Export maps those ranges into accepted or proposed output before rendering:

```tsx
const result = await exportDocx(editor, {
  comments,
  projection: "review",
});
```

The same configured static renderer handles the main document and rich comment bodies. Under the default `lossPolicy`, a comment without a complete range fails the export; with `lossPolicy: "allow"` the export omits it with a warning.

### Static rendering and styles

DOCX owns its semantic static mappings for code blocks, columns, equations,
callouts, headings, and tables of contents. They set no color, font, or font
size beyond an element's own values, such as a callout's color. The app's
`stylesheet` sets them and can select:

- `editor-<plugin name>`, the class on each drawn element, such as `.editor-codeBlock`;
- `table > tbody > tr > td` inside a callout, whose first cell holds the icon;
- `[data-docx-preserve-whitespace]`, the text of a code block;
- `[data-empty]`, the placeholder of an empty equation or table of contents.

The top and bottom margin, in `pt` or `px`, of a paragraph, heading or other
block drawn as one Word paragraph sets its Word space before and after. A margin
on a list, list item or table does not reach the file. Without a margin, the
Word paragraph and heading styles set that spacing.

What the `stylesheet` adds to a run, such as a heading's size or a header
cell's bold, exports as a Word character style named `Plate Look`. Runs that
need a different look get `Plate Look 2`, `Plate Look 3` and so on. Each style
leaves out what the paragraph's Word style already sets, because Word turns
bold off when two style levels both turn it on. Direct run formatting in the
file holds only a node's own marks and style. When a custom `component` puts a
span inside `strong` or `b`, the content after that element in the same parent
also exports as direct bold. Paragraph alignment, spacing and line height
export as direct paragraph formatting.

Each paragraph of a blockquote takes Word's `Quote` paragraph style. Inline
code and keyboard text take the `HTML Code`, `HTML Keyboard` and
`HTML Code Keyboard` character styles. These styles carry the stylesheet's
`blockquote`, `code` and `kbd` rules. A code or keyboard run gets a numbered
copy of its style, such as `HTML Code 2`, when it also sits in a link, when
the stylesheet gives it a look its style lacks, or when both its style and its
paragraph style are bold. Import reads these styles and their numbered copies
back as a blockquote and as code and kbd marks. A numbered copy whose name
element also declares a namespace prefix named `val` imports as plain text
with a warning. A `Plate Look` style adds no mark, so a heading or header cell
comes back without a bold mark.

The app may pass a custom root `component` when it needs a different wrapper.
`DOCX_EXPORT_STYLES` is the registry's copied presentation preset.

Equations export as Word math built from KaTeX's MathML. An equation that uses
notation the converter does not cover, such as a matrix, exports its TeX as
text inside a Word equation. A task list item, an `li` with `data-checked`, exports with a ☑ or ☐
list marker, and import reads a bullet level drawn as ☐, ☑ or ☒ as a task item with
its checked state.

A live editor's components are editing UI. Pass the app's static kit as
`presentation` so the body and every comment body draw with static components
of the same plugin name. A plugin with no static version exports its content
without its component or wrapper and reports a `missing-static-presentation`
diagnostic, which counts as lost content under `lossPolicy`.

<ComponentSource name="docx-export" />

## Format support

| Content                                                            | Behavior                                                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Paragraphs, headings, supported marks and properties               | Decode through the installed HTML mappings and export through the configured static renderer                |
| Lists, links, bookmarks, rows, cells, and spans                    | Preserve supported semantic structure; omit Word layout details with diagnostics                            |
| Tracked revisions                                                  | Preserve supported insert, delete, move, run-property, and paragraph-property changes when `plugins` include `AuthoredPlugin`; otherwise flatten with a `lossy-content` warning |
| Main-body comments                                                 | Preserve rich bodies, ranges, author/date metadata, durable IDs, replies, and resolved state when present   |
| Embedded images during import                                      | Omit the resource and return a `resource-omitted` diagnostic                                                |
| Links and images during export                                     | Write absolute navigation-safe links and PNG, JPEG, GIF, or BMP images; report removed destinations and omitted images |
| Single-section headers and footers                                | Keep safe closed source subgraphs during source-aware export; do not map them into the editor document. Import reports one that holds content as dropped |
| Multiple-section headers and footers, footnotes, and endnotes     | Inspect and bound their package parts; omit them from edited source-aware output with diagnostics. Import reports them as dropped content, except a part that draws nothing, such as one that holds only Word's separator notes or an empty styled paragraph |
| Fields, text boxes, shapes, charts, SmartArt, and embedded objects | Keep reachable plain text when conversion exposes it; report unsupported or omitted content                 |
| Plate named roots and metadata                                     | Omit from Word output with a `lossy-content` warning; keep them in authored JSON                            |
| Equations                                                          | Export as Word math, or as TeX inside a Word equation when the converter does not cover the notation. Import reads Word math into `equation` and `inlineEquation` nodes, with TeX rebuilt from the equation's structure, or into TeX text when those plugins are not installed. A display equation that shares its paragraph with a comment, tracked change or text, or sits in a list item or an indented paragraph, imports inline |
| Task lists                                                         | Give an item drawn as an `li` with `data-checked` a ☑ or ☐ list marker; import a bullet level drawn as ☐, ☑ or ☒ as a task item with its checked state |
| Custom Plate, emoji, and media nodes                               | Use their configured static serializers; unsupported output produces diagnostics                            |

## API reference

### Entrypoints

| Surface                         | Import path           |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| `WordPastePlugin`               | `platejs/docx/paste`  |
| `importDocx` and import types   | `platejs/docx/import` |
| `exportDocx` and export types | `platejs/docx/export` |

### `importDocx`

```ts
importDocx(
  source: ArrayBuffer | Blob,
  options: DocxImportOptions
): Promise<DocxImportResult>
```

`DocxImportOptions` requires `plugins` and accepts `schema`, a cooperative
`signal`, `lossPolicy`, partial `limits` overrides, and
`retainSource`. Import captures that detached target before its first await and
never reads a live editor or mutable plugin store. Every limit must be a
positive safe integer. Literal `retainSource: true` conditionally adds
`source: DocxSource | null` to a successful result; omitted or literal `false`
does not.

| Default limit      |   Value |
| ------------------ | ------: |
| `maxInputBytes`    |  32 MiB |
| `maxEntries`       |   1,024 |
| `maxEntryBytes`    |  32 MiB |
| `maxExpandedBytes` | 128 MiB |
| `maxRelationships` |   4,096 |
| `maxRevisions`     |   2,000 |
| `maxComments`      |   5,000 |
| `maxXmlDepth`      |     128 |
| `maxXmlNodes`      | 500,000 |

A successful result contains `document`, `comments`, and structured `diagnostics`. A retained success also contains `source`, which is `null` when exact export cannot admit the package. A failed result contains only `diagnostics`.

### `DocxSource`

```ts
class DocxSource {
  private constructor();
  dispose(): void;
}
```

Only `importDocx` creates a `DocxSource`. The object is process-local and cannot be serialized or reattached. To restore source-aware export after application reload, keep the original file and import it again.

### `exportDocx`

```ts
exportDocx(
  editor: Editor,
  options: DocxExportOptions
): Promise<DocxExportResult>
```

| Option                  | Type                                     | Description                                        |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `projection`            | `'accepted' \| 'proposed' \| 'review'`   | Required visible document projection               |
| `comments`              | `readonly DocxComment[]`                 | Comment records whose ranges address the stored document |
| `allowRemoteImages`     | `boolean`                                | Fetch remote HTTP(S) images to embed; defaults to `false` |
| `component`             | `React.ComponentType<EditorStaticProps>` | Optional static editor wrapper                     |
| `fontFamily`            | `string`                                 | Document body font; rebuilds an unchanged `source` |
| `lossPolicy`            | `'allow' \| 'reject'`                    | Fail on dropped content, or return it as warnings; defaults to `'reject'` |
| `margins`               | `Margins`                                | Page margins in twentieths of a point              |
| `orientation`           | `'landscape' \| 'portrait'`              | Page orientation                                   |
| `pageSize`              | `PageSize`                               | Page dimensions in twentieths of a point           |
| `presentation`          | `readonly BasePluginInput[]`             | Static plugins that draw the body and comment bodies, matched by plugin name |
| `signal`                | `AbortSignal`                            | Cooperative cancellation signal                    |
| `source`                | `DocxSource \| null`                     | Retained source correspondence for exact or safe export |
| `stylesheet`            | `string`                                 | CSS that sets the Word look; rebuilds an unchanged `source` |
| `title`                 | `string`                                 | Document metadata title                            |

A successful result contains `blob` and structured `diagnostics`. A failed result contains only `diagnostics`.

See [Authored Changes](/docs/plate/authored-changes) for the editor's accepted, proposed, and review projections.
